<chapter id="matrixref">
#title("Linear Matrices")

#para("\
\
#emphasis("Note:") the first versions of this chapter described persistent
matrices (the type #stacode("matrixref")), which are reclaimed by garbage
collection (GC). That type was removed when the support for GC was dropped:
the compiler now rejects every nonlinear heap allocation. The matrices that
are allocated on heap are linear now: the type for them is
#stacode("matrixptr") and they must be freed explicitly. The interface is in
#myatscodelink("prelude/SATS/matrixptr.sats", "prelude/SATS/matrixptr.sats").
\
")

#para("\

A matrix of dimension m by n is just an array of size
m*n. Like in C, the representation of a matrix in ATS is row-major. In
other words, element (i, j) in a matrix of dimension m by n is element
i*n+j in the underlying array that represents the matrix.

")

#para("\

Given a viewtype VT and two integers M and N, the type
#stacode("matrixptr(VT, M, N)") is for linear matrices of dimension M
by N that contain elements of the viewtype VT.  There is no dimension
information attached to matrixptr-values at run-time: the dimensions are
part of the static type only. The interfaces for various
functions on linear matrices can be found in the SATS file
#myatscodelink("prelude/SATS/matrixptr.sats",
"prelude/SATS/matrixptr.sats"), which is automatically loaded by
#command("patsopt").

")#comment("para")

#para('\

The following function is commonly used to create a matrixptr-value:

#sats2xhtml('\
fun{
a:t0p
} matrixptr_make_elt
  {m,n:int}
  (m: size_t m, n: size_t n, x: a):<!wrt> matrixptr(a, m, n)
// end of [matrixptr_make_elt]
')

Given two sizes m and n plus an element x,
#dyncode("matrixptr_make_elt") returns a matrix of dimension m by n
in which each cell is initialized with the element x. The matrix is
released by calling the function #dyncode("matrixptr_free"):

#sats2xhtml('\
fun matrixptr_free
  {a:t0p}{l:addr}{m,n:int}
  (A: matrixptr(INV(a), l, m, n)):<!wrt> void = "mac#%"
// end of [matrixptr_free]
')

')

#para('\

Also, the following function can be called to turn an array into
a matrix (the array is consumed):

#sats2xhtml('\
fun{
} arrayptr2matrixptr_size
  {a:vt0p}{l:addr}{m,n:int}
  (A: arrayptr(INV(a), l, m*n), m: size_t m, n: size_t n):<> matrixptr(a, l, m, n)
')

')

#para('\

For accessing and updating the content of a matrix-cell, the following
two functions #dyncode("matrixptr_get_at") and #dyncode("matrixptr_set_at")
can be called (we show the ones for the indices of the type
#stacode("size_t")):

#sats2xhtml('\
fun{a:t0p}
matrixptr_get_at_size
  {m,n:int}
(
  A: !matrixptr(INV(a), m, n), i: sizeLt (m), n: size_t n, j: sizeLt (n)
) :<> (a) // end of [matrixptr_get_at_size]
//
fun{a:t0p}
matrixptr_set_at_size
  {m,n:int}
(
  A: !matrixptr(INV(a), m, n), i: sizeLt (m), n: size_t n, j: sizeLt (n), x: a
) :<!wrt> void // end of [matrixptr_set_at_size]
')

Note that it is not enough to just supply the coordinates of a
matrix-cell in order to access it; the column dimension of the matrix
needs to be supplied as well. The symbol #keycode("!") in front of the
type of the matrix indicates that the matrix is only borrowed; it is
still available after the call.

')

#para('\

In the following presentation, I give an implementation of a function that
turns a given square matrix into its transpose:

#dats2xhtml('\
extern
fun{a:t0p}
matrixptr_transpose
  {n:nat}
(
  M: !matrixptr(a, n, n), n: size_t(n)
) : void // end of [matrixptr_transpose]
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
matrixptr_transpose
  {n}(M, n) = let
//
fun
loop
{i,j:nat |
 i < j; j <= n
} .<n-i,n-j>.
(
 M: !matrixptr(a, n, n), i: size_t(i), j: size_t(j)
) : void = (
//
if
(j < n)
then let
  val x = matrixptr_get_at(M, i, n, j)
  val y = matrixptr_get_at(M, j, n, i)
  val () = matrixptr_set_at(M, i, n, j, y)
  val () = matrixptr_set_at(M, j, n, i, x)
in
  loop (M, i, succ(j))
end // end of [then]
else let
  val i1 = succ (i)
in
//
if i1 < n then loop(M, i1, succ(i1)) else ()
//
end // end of [else]
//
) (* end of [loop] *)
//
in
  if (n > 0) then loop(M, i2sz(0), i2sz(1)) else ()
end // end of [matrixptr_transpose]
')

Note that the nested function #dyncode("loop") takes the matrix as an
argument instead of capturing it: a linear value cannot be captured by a
function that may be called more than once.

')#comment("para")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("chap_matrixptr.dats", "on-line") the entirety of the
code used in this chapter.\

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--"matrixref"-->

